      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bryce_Canyon_National_Park",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The park was named after Ebenezer Bryce, a 1874 homesteader, after Mormon pioneers settled the area in the 1850s.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bryce_Canyon_National_Park",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "President Warren G. Harding declared the area a national monument in 1923, and Congress redesignated it a national park in 1928.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5,
        24
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bryce_Canyon_National_Park",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Early 20th‑century Union Pacific and Santa Fe railroads promoted the canyon in 1916 magazine articles, sparking tourist interest.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        9
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bryce_Canyon_National_Park",
      "category": "culture",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Bryce Canyon’s hoodoos form from frost weathering and stream erosion of sedimentary rock, creating the park’s signature spires.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        20
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bryce_Canyon_National_Park",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "The park spans three life zones, supporting 1,000 plant species, 59 mammal species, and over 100 bird species.",
